{"title":"Continuity","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The notion of <b>Continuity</b> is one of the most important in real analysis, partly because continous functions most closely resemble the behaviour of observables in nature.\n</p><p>Although continuity and continous functions can be defined on more general sets, we will first restrict ourselves to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906fddead65545297c74bbc2ca83cd01f3f9ecc9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbb{R}$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition\">Definition</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Continuity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Definition\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/c/4fc367d27a247f0657c3dc353e448f804cdeaf29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A\\subset\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/e/7eea905af8c70e71e7aa957e63cca30cd2d796f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:A\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"80\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/645ce1d6ed9b85b13d81016a6f37c0c4297a5594.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>We say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is continous at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/2/972acc638fbe32d5ce85671e73b84dc29416e4da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\forall\\varepsilon&gt;0\\;\\exists\\delta&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"108\" height=\"13\" /> such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/6550276377d461aa90435ac0d2f08dfbef079a54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/a/dba681811de2ac06c875452c7bcc9fbdcd85ac30.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[|x-c|&lt;\\delta\\Rightarrow |f(c)-f(x)|&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]\" width=\"246\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is continous at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/645ce1d6ed9b85b13d81016a6f37c0c4297a5594.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" />, we say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is <b>continous over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /></b>.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition_for_metric_spaces\">Definition for metric spaces</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Continuity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Definition for metric spaces\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can easily extend this definition to <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Metric_space\" title=\"Metric space\">metric spaces</a>. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be metric spaces. Given a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7f10cd1eb1a70f757d68726825511ffa17c6ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:X\\to Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"16\" />, and a point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/b/eeb25ebd76a375ab3aa7f5200fd2de12d254bcaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\in X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" />, we say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is continuous a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> if, for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/e/4aedf4f18c620d464565f8a52547a478421a97dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varepsilon &gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/d/c6da49fefb1906c55ed166a63282b0d8e19ca31c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\delta&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/8/e789763cfcc6a3688812b3660fda9b6d9cc80a4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\in X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"13\" />,<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/9/bb961d1390a69d2149197abd24b5ceac16e4bc2b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[d_X(c,x)&lt;\\delta\\Rightarrow d_Y(f(c),f(x))&lt;\\varepsilon.\\]\" width=\"271\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is continous at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/b/eeb25ebd76a375ab3aa7f5200fd2de12d254bcaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c\\in X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" />, we say that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/2/bb2c93730dbb48558bb3c4738c956c4e8f816437.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> is <b>continous over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /></b>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Definition_for_Topological_spaces\">Definition for Topological spaces</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Continuity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Definition for Topological spaces\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Perhaps the most general definition of continuity is in the context of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Topological_space\" title=\"Topological space\">topological spaces</a>. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are topological spaces, then a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/7/5a7f10cd1eb1a70f757d68726825511ffa17c6ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f:X\\to Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"85\" height=\"16\" /> is called continuous if for any open set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/d/22dd4bec240494a23e2552ca6cfcb9c43db7245f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{U}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, it's <i>preimage</i> (i.e. the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/9/5898c3cb19d3b2bfe3bf5264bf1ebf9dce296558.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f^{-1}(\\mathcal{U}) = \\{x\\in X|f(x)\\in\\mathcal{U}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"222\" height=\"19\" />) is an open set in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />. Note that the image of an open set in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> does <b>not</b> have to be open.\n</p><p>It can be shown that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/4/6a47ca0fe7cb276abc022af6ac88ddae1a9d6894.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/5/ce58e4af225c93d08606c26554caaa5ae32edeba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are metric spaces under the metric space topology, that this definition of continuity coincides with the previous one.\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Continuity&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018081529\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.019 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.030 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 191/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 271/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6934/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.518      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.518      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:6425:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018081529 and revision id 73434. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}